THE AUDIT CALLS SIX LAWFUL NODES MISSING RULES

Run this and read the AUDIT MISSRULE lines. Six of them name Grokking members
that were minted as bare literals:

    strap  = grok += new("leftBrace");
    text   = "[";

They carry no rStuff, and THAT IS THE DESIGN, in Tony's words on 2026-08-28:
rules added to Grokking do not carry rStuff; the rStuff gets added when those
literal rules are added to other rules in the body of GroupMain, either by a
setRuleStuff call or by a modify call which then runs setRuleStuff.

So nothing is wrong with GroupMain. The audit is asking a question the answer
to which is not a defect.

WHY THE AUDIT SEES THEM AT ALL, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING

`auditMissingRules` tests `isRule && !rStuff`. The master never gets rStuff --
correct. But it DOES get isRule, and it gets it retroactively, from a node that
is not itself:

  1. `grok += new("leftBrace")` mints the master. No isRule, no rStuff.
  2. `strap +% grok/leftBrace` -- addGroup sees the node already has a parent,
     so it COPIES it: `if (group->parent) group = new GroupItem(group);`
  3. that copy constructor SHARES the body -- `groupBody = grup->groupBody` --
     while rStuff is per-node and starts null.
  4. `modify(copy,"-")` calls `copy->setRuleStuff()`, and setRuleStuff's first
     act is `groupBody->flags.isRule = 1` when the registry is a rule registry.

Step 4 writes the SHARED body. So the copy gets its own rStuff and the MASTER
gets isRule. The master now reads isRule 1, rStuff 0 -- the audit's exact
signature -- without anything ever having been done to the master.

⚠ THIS IS RULING D'S DIAGNOSTIC, WORD FOR WORD: the defect is a question posed
to the wrong oracle. `isRule` lives in groupBody, which is COPIED (here,
shared). `rStuff` is never copied. Whenever a guard and its subject live in
different structures, ask whether they can disagree. Here they do, and the
audit is the place it becomes visible rather than the place it happens.

THE NEGATIVE CONTROL, RUN, AND IT IS WHAT PROVES THE ABOVE

A seventh master `zzNeverUsed` was minted exactly like the six -- same
`grok += new(...)`, same text -- and referenced by NO rule. Built and run:

    the six, each referenced by a rule      AUDIT MISSRULE, all six
    zzNeverUsed, referenced by nothing      ABSENT from the audit entirely

Minting is clean. Being ATTACHED is what marks the master. The control was
removed after measuring; it is not in the tree.

⚠ AND THE SAME MECHANISM IS WHY THE ODOMETER GREW. genParse's population
filter is `if !isRulE; continue;`, so the six pass it on the back-propagated
flag, enter the plan population, and refuse for want of the rStuff they are
not supposed to have: "rule-level literal but no rStuff, so LIT vs LITTO is
undecidable". Population 58 -> 64, red 39 -> 45. Same one fact, second
instrument. odometer.base is re-pinned to that; the audit is NOT, pending the
ruling below.

⚠ A DEAD END ALREADY WALKED, so nobody walks it twice: adding setRuleStuff()
to the six masters DOES clear both rows -- audit back to 4, odometer 25 green
-- and it is WRONG, because it gives the masters an rStuff the design says
they must not have. It was tried, measured green, and reverted on Tony's word.
Green is not the test here.

NEXT: RULED -- THE SHAPE IS TONY'S, AND NOTHING BELOW IS BUILT

Three shapes, and the choice is a ruling rather than a measurement, because all
three produce a green fleet and they disagree about what is true.

  A. TEACH THE AUDIT THE CATEGORY. A literal master is distinguishable per-node
     without consulting the shared flag -- it has data, no groupList, and its
     parent is the registry rather than a rule. Exempt that shape from
     MISSRULE. Cheapest, and it leaves the flag lying.

  B. STOP THE BACK-PROPAGATION. setRuleStuff would raise isRule somewhere
     per-node rather than in groupBody. Correct at the root and the only one
     that also fixes the odometer without a second edit. ⚠ BLAST RADIUS
     UNMEASURED AND LARGE: isRule is read across the tree and this changes what
     it means for every copied node, not just these six.

  C. ACCEPT AND RE-PIN. Declare 10 the right number and let the six sit in the
     rule population. Costs nothing today and makes the audit permanently
     unable to distinguish a literal master from a rule someone forgot to
     finish -- which is the thing the audit exists to catch.

WHAT WOULD MAKE THE CHOICE CHEAPER: a census of `isRule` reads that could see a
shared-body node, which is the only unknown standing between B and the others.
Not run.
